So what? There's plenty of specialized (non-"joker") substitutes,
plus the optional six pieces of non-body armor that add to your AC.

> Tell me how do you normally manage AC in the early to midgame? It's
> usually my most critical issue, closely followed by MR, reflection and
> poison resistance.

Well, my ranking is somewhat different, more level specific. MR is
not crucial until you reach around Medusa/Castle, though, starting
around level 9 I feel better with MR (or polymorph control) because
of the polytraps. Reflection I'd like to have earlier (around level
15 or so) when more of the intelligent zappers appear; I wouldn't
ever wake up the inhabitants of barracks if I have no reflection.
OTOH, it's not uncommon that I still lack reflection by the Castle;
it's not crucial (I even ascended without). The most important early
game resistance is poison resistance; around level 5+, when orcs and
spiked pits may appear, it requires some more attention if you don't
have it.

How I manage my AC very much depends on the random findings, on the
class played, and more factors. Usually I take the best armor I find
(pet tested), as long as it fits to the situation. Currently I play
a wizard that resorted to mithril and GoPs, thus reducing his ad-hoc
spell casting capabilities (must undress somewhat, the GoPs, to cast
his low level spells), and uses his elven dagger stack, instead, for
ranged attacks. If I see that I cannot manage some type of creature
I retreat or let my pet(s) do the job. I think I've also bought some
intrinsic AC of ~3 in that game.

But I am not focussed on AC alone; if I lack some adequate AC I try
to obtain some effective ranged attacks, for example. Nethack allows
a bunch of ways to reach the goal (or any sub-goal).

